cartridge "TANK AMBUSH/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1989"

' TANK AMBUSH
' A ColecoVision game based on "Tank Ambush" by Anthony Bertram, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette February 1989 p. 26 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2062).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the article's and the original's
' (run in jsc64 and read from its machine code, orig/tankamb.dis), rebuilt
' for the ColecoVision: a smooth-turning horizon, enemy sprites in three
' sizes, sounds, a tune.
'
' The game: you sit in a tank in the desert, out of fuel. Enemy soldiers
' and jeeps come in from the horizon on every side. Left/right swivel the
' turret all the way round (the ridge line and the enemies slide past);
' up/down raise and lower the gun: the higher the gun, the farther the
' shell bursts. A shell that bursts on an enemy destroys it (25 points)
' and a new one appears on the horizon. An enemy that gets close fires:
' his ammo bursts in front of your tank (a warning); one that reaches the
' tank destroys it. Three tanks. Levels 1-3 set the enemies' speed.
'
' From the original (orig/tankamb.dis, timed in jsc64 with
' orig/c64speed-tambush.mjs, about 768 main-loop passes a second, 12.8 a
' frame): four enemies (two soldiers, two jeeps) start on the horizon at
' fixed places round the tank; all of them step 1 pixel nearer every N
' passes, N = 256 / 215 / 185 for levels 1-3 (here 13 passes are counted
' a frame: level 1 crosses the 80 pixels of desert in about 26 s, as
' measured); each kill makes N one smaller (faster), losing a tank sets N
' half way back to the start. The gun's elevation steps every 150 passes
' (12 frames), 19 steps; the burst lands 8 + 4 x step pixels from the
' horizon. A shot flies 56 passes and bursts for 200 (4 and 15 frames);
' while it is out the turret can't turn or tilt. The button is taken at
' most every 512 passes (40 frames). An enemy hit explodes for 510 passes
' (40 frames), during which the others stand still, then comes back on
' the horizon at its own starting place round the tank. Every 1024 passes
' (80 frames) the game checks the nearest enemy: 45 pixels from the
' horizon or more, a burst in front of the tank (23 frames); 75 or more,
' the tank is hit (83 frames), all enemies go back to the horizon. Score
' 25 a kill, six digits.
'
' Liberties: the C64 view wrapped sprites over a 512-pixel circle with far
' enemies sliding three times faster than near ones as you turned; here
' the world is a 1024-pixel circle and everything slides together, 3
' pixels a frame (a full turn in under 6 s), with the ridge line scrolling
' pixel by pixel. A small range mark shows where the shell will burst (the
' C64 showed only the barrel's height). The warning burst appears on the
' side of the nearest enemy (the C64 picked a side at random), a fair hint
' for enemies behind you. Enemies come back with a random shift of up to
' 32 pixels round the circle (the C64 used the same four places every
' time). Everything stands still while your tank explodes (the C64 kept
' the other enemies moving). The enemies' speed never goes above 40
' passes a step (the C64's counter would wrap and slow down again after
' 185+ kills at level 3). The button fires at once when 40 frames have
' passed since the last shot (the C64 sampled it every 40 frames). The
' C64 used SHIFT to pause; there is no pause here. Hits: the C64 used the
' sprite collision of the burst with an enemy; here a box: the enemy's
' centre within 4/7/10 pixels (far/middle/near) of the gun's line and
' within 4 pixels of the burst's distance. Added: a title tune,
' a credits page, sound effects for the turret, the gun and the bursts.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): left/right turn the turret, up raises the
' gun (farther), down lowers it (nearer), either button fires. On the
' title, up and down choose the level, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

